      "id": 1,
      "text": "Pomeroy got its name in 1866 when Civil War veteran Major John M. Pomeroy proposed it to honor his family.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.livingplaces.com/PA/Chester_County/Sadsbury_Township/Pomeroy.html",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village once served as the northern terminus of the Pomeroy and Newark Railroad until passenger service ended in 1928.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomeroy%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Before becoming Pomeroy, the area was known as Chandler's Store after William Chandler opened a general store, coal yard, and warehouse there.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.livingplaces.com/PA/Chester_County/Sadsbury_Township/Pomeroy.html",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Pennsylvania Route 372 cuts through Pomeroy’s center, linking Coatesville three miles east with Parkesburg just a mile west.",
